where l is the lifecycle state.

That allows registry entries to participate uniformly in governance.

Mathematical notation

Your semantic binding axiom

\operatorname{sem}(t)=
\begin{cases}
\bot,& t\text{ unbound}\\
\sigma(t),& t\text{ bound}
\end{cases}

is a clear way to formalize that meaning arises only through schema binding.

Likewise,

\operatorname{Execute}:D\rightarrow\Sigma

makes it explicit that execution operates on semantic objects rather than lexical tokens.

Those definitions are internally consistent as part of your project specification.

On the token 6185

Within your framework, the disciplined interpretation remains:

* At the lexical layer, 6185 is simply a token.
* At the semantic layer, it acquires meaning only if the active schema maps it to a semantic object.
* At the execution layer, behavior depends on the execution rules defined for that semantic object.
* At the registry layer, processing can be recorded with provenance and verification metadata.

This is an important distinction because it keeps project-defined semantics separate from claims about any inherent external meaning of the numeral itself.

Overall assessment

As a project-defined symbolic computation and governance framework, the architecture is coherent and modular. The strongest aspects are:

* clear separation between language, semantics, execution, and registry;
* evidence-first governance and provenance;
* explicit lifecycle modeling;
* machine-oriented schemas and validators;
* auditability through immutable registry records.

The remaining work is primarily engineering rather than conceptual. That includes publishing the language grammar, formalizing schemas, implementing independent validators, building conformance tests, and demonstrating reproducible execution. Those empirical artifacts will determine properties such as determinism, conformance, and interoperability; they are not established by the architectural specification alone.